<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Worms</h1><p class="st-meta">1184 CE – 1797 CE (approx.) · capital Worms</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Rhine city of the Nibelungenlied, the Concordat of 1122 and Luther's defiance at the Diet of 1521, home of one of Europe's oldest Jewish communities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Worms was a Roman and Frankish centre, seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and in legend of the Burgundian kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>. Its citizens won growing privileges from the Salian emperors, who signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concordat-of-worms">Concordat of Worms</a> there in 1122. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa's</a> charter of 1184 confirmed the city's freedoms, and it gradually threw off the bishop's lordship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ruled, frequently in conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Worms hosted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diets</a>, most famously in 1521, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> refused to recant before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. Its Jewish community, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schum-cities">SchUM</a> centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> learning, had a synagogue and cemetery dating from the 11th century. The city adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops burned Worms in 1689, and it never fully recovered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">Revolutionary France</a> annexed it in 1797. In 1816 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a>, and it is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schum-cities">SchUM</a> sites are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-worms.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Worms" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Struppig taucher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nibelungenbr%C3%BCcke_mit_Dom,_Rhein,_Rheinpromenade_und_Innenstadt_Worms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1184 CE – 1797 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Worms</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Worms</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1490&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-worms" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Worms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4872 449 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Worms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5192 705L5229 694M5192 705L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5192 705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5192 705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1490&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-worms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

